<h2>How to choose heated socks for skiing</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Battery life is the real spec.</strong> Most pairs in Outdoor Life's test lasted about three hours on high. Running them on a lower setting stretches that a lot, and warmer, wool-heavy socks need less heat in the first place.</li>
  <li><strong>Fit inside a ski boot.</strong> A thin sock with the battery at the cuff, above the boot, is the comfortable setup. Bulky socks change how your boot fits.</li>
  <li><strong>Controls you can reach.</strong> Buttons get buried under base layers and ski pants. Outdoor Life prefers easy manual controls or a phone app.</li>
  <li><strong>Washing.</strong> Remove the batteries, cover the plug, use the mesh bag on a delicate cycle and hang to dry (Field &amp; Stream).</li>
  <li><strong>Don't rely on them alone.</strong> "Any battery powered device is subject to fail," Outdoor Life notes, so still layer properly. See <%= link_to "the best ski socks", guide_path("best-ski-socks") %> and <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Flying with them:</strong> TSA requires spare lithium batteries to go in carry-on bags only, with a 100 watt-hour limit per battery.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cold hands too? See <%= link_to "the best heated gloves", guide_path("best-heated-gloves") %> and <%= link_to "the best hand warmers", guide_path("best-hand-warmers") %>.</p>

